    <section id="overview" class="mb-4">
      <h2 class="h4">AppSec Guru</h2>
      <p>A cockpit around <b>Tenable Security Center</b> and its <b>WAS (Web Application Scanning)</b> sensors, plus
        content discovery (ffuf), source review (SAST) and an offensive AI assistant. It drives real scanners and
        starts real scans, so only point it at systems you are authorised to test.</p>
      <p class="text-body-secondary">A Tenable Cyber Exchange project by Ali Okan Yüksel ·
        <a href="https://buymeacoffee.com/aliokanyuksel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">buy me a coffee</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="pipeline" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">How a scan works</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>You pick a target, a source policy and plugin families in <a href="/create-scan">Create Scan</a> (or save them as a profile in <a href="/scans">Scans</a>).</li>
        <li>The cockpit <b>copies</b> the policy (the original is never changed), enables the families and writes the scope preferences.</li>
        <li>It launches the scan on Security Center, which hands it to a WAS sensor.</li>
        <li>It opens an ssh stream to that sensor and captures <b>only this scan's</b> log lines (matched by the <code>[scanUuid=…]</code> tag) into a file and per-minute charts.</li>
        <li>When the scan finishes it pulls the findings, reverts the debug level and (unless you kept them) deletes the temporary scan and policy copy.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="scope" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">Scope &amp; the <span class="badge text-bg-primary">Fast</span> preset</h3>
      <p>Scope controls how much of the site the crawler covers and how long it may run. The three choices in the
        wizard map to policy preferences:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Fast</b> — a built-in preset defined in the tool (not a Security Center setting). It overrides the copied policy with the values below so a test scan finishes in minutes.</li>
        <li><b>As in the policy</b> — the copied policy's own limits are kept untouched.</li>
        <li><b>Custom</b> — you set the page limit, time limit and request rate directly.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-1">The <b>Fast</b> preset writes these WAS preferences:</p>
      <div class="table-responsive"><table class="table table-sm">
        <thead><tr><th>Preference</th><th>Fast value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">was[scope]page_limit</td><td>150</td><td>max pages crawled</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">was[scope]directory_depth_limit</td><td>4</td><td>how deep into directories</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">was[scope]dom_depth_limit</td><td>3</td><td>how deep into the DOM / SPA</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">was_timeout</td><td>00:45:00</td><td>overall time limit</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">was[http]request_concurrency</td><td>10</td><td>parallel requests</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">was[assessment][bruteforce]enable</td><td>no</td><td>login bruteforce stays off</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
      <p class="small text-body-secondary">Source: the <code>FAST_PREFS</code> map in the tool's WAS helpers
        (<code>api/wastools/core.py</code>, shared with the <code>tsc-was-debug-scanctl.py</code> CLI). Edit it there to change the preset.
        A full WAS scan with no preset uses the policy's defaults (typically 10 000 pages, 8-hour timeout).</p>
    </section>

    <section id="families" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">Plugin families</h3>
      <p>WAS groups its checks into 14 families (Authentication &amp; Session, Injection, Cross Site Scripting,
        SSL/TLS, HTTP Security Header, Data Exposure, and so on). Enabling fewer families makes a scan finish
        faster and keeps the debug log readable. If you select none, the copied policy's own families run.
        The <b>Policy</b> step in the wizard shows which families each policy already has and lets you compare them.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="debug" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">Debug &amp; logs</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Policy debug</b> sets <code>was_debug_mode=yes</code> in the copied policy: the scan writes extra plugin output. No service restart.</li>
        <li><b>Scanner-host debug</b> raises the sensor's <code>WAS_LOG_LEVEL</code> to DEBUG. This restarts the scanner service, so it is refused while another scan is running, and it is reverted when the scan ends.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/logs">Logs</a> page streams the sensor log live (Server-Sent Events) filtered to one scan, with
        level toggles, quick filters (page audits, plugins, crawl, throttle, warnings, lifecycle) and per-10-second
        charts. Every run also keeps its captured log on the <a href="/scan-results">Scan Results</a> detail page.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="fuzz" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">Hidden Path Discovery</h3>
      <p>Content discovery with <a href="https://github.com/ffuf/ffuf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ffuf</a>: it requests
        every word from a wordlist against the target and keeps the responses whose status code matches. Put
        <code>FUZZ</code> where paths should be tried, or leave it off to append <code>/FUZZ</code>. Three wordlists ship
        built in (common paths, API endpoints, backup files); you can upload your own. Use <b>filter codes</b> to drop a
        catch-all status (single-page apps often return 200 for everything).</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sast" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">SAST &amp; the AI harness</h3>
      <p>The <a href="/sast-scan">SAST Scan</a> clones a Git repository, extracts every function with its parameters and
        the dangerous sinks it reaches, ranks them, and the <b>offensive-security agent</b> reviews each argument from
        source to sink with the local LLM, using tools to follow callees and sanitizers. The agent's behaviour is defined
        in Markdown under <code>ai/</code> (<code>agents/</code>, <code>skills/</code>, <code>tools/</code>), read at run time —
        edit a file and the next run uses it. Function-level review suits structured code; vulnerabilities written as
        top-level script statements may not be captured as functions.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">AI analysis &amp; assistant</h3>
      <p>On a finished run, <b>Analyze with AI</b> runs the offensive-security agent over the findings. It returns attack
        paths, categories, a reconnaissance table built from <i>informational</i> plugins (detected technologies, exposed
        tokens, API endpoints) and quick wins, and renders a standalone <b>HTML report</b>. The <b>AI assistant</b> chat
        (top-right on every page) talks to the same Ollama model; attach the on-screen log, WARN/ERROR only, or the scan
        status and ask about it. Both need an Ollama URL and model in <a href="/settings">Settings</a>; a model that lists
        <code>tools</code> works best, but a plain model still works in JSON mode.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sensors" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">Sensors &amp; settings</h3>
      <p>One Security Center, any number of WAS scanner hosts. Each <a href="/sensors">sensor</a> is a reusable ssh
        profile (host, credentials, log path, optional jump host). A scan profile can pick a sensor; otherwise the
        default sensor is used. All connection details come from <code>.env</code> first and can be overridden and tested
        in <a href="/settings">Settings</a>; secrets are stored encrypted.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="api" class="mb-4">
      <h3 class="h5">REST API</h3>
      <p>Everything here is backed by the <code>/api</code> REST API. The <a href="/devops">DevOps</a> page has copy-paste
        <code>curl</code> recipes for starting scans, listing running scans, fetching logs, running the AI analysis,
        discovery and SAST. The interactive API docs are at <a href="/api/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/docs</a>.</p>
    </section>

